  "slug": "untitled-topic-29c0",
  "summary": "New preprints remove transcript spillover in imaging spatial data, attach confidence to histology-based expression inference and noise-correct benchmarks.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is spatial and single-cell modeling that explicitly separates technical artifact from biological signal. In imaging-based spatial transcriptomics, Dancer uses a reference-guided probabilistic framework to distinguish within-cell-type heterogeneity from transcript contamination. SYNDRI, a transformer trained on simulated data, removes transcript spillover. For histology-to-expression prediction, REGAIN attaches per-prediction confidence and reports gains in cell-type annotation and prognostic value in TCGA-BRCA. A noise-corrected correlation metric (RSC) is proposed for benchmarking such predictions. A second strand targets integration and imputation: scOnion scores cell-level RNA–chromatin concordance, scRefine applies hierarchical contrastive learning to atlas integration and rare-population annotation, BRIDGE performs contrastive cross-tissue imputation on GTEx, and GASTON-VI learns spatial axes across samples and modalities. Network and state-level analyses (CHO iModulons, CAR T gene-dependency spectra, UCE state bridges, lung adenocarcinoma Global Tumor Time) are adjacent. All are preprints, so reported gains remain unreviewed.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
